/* Module 4 — The Cytoskeleton
   Source: BIOL 8420 Module 4 Practice Quiz (instructor-highlighted answers)
   opts format: [text, isCorrect, whyLine] */
window.BANK_M2 = [
{n:1, topic:"Microtubule structure", stem:"The alpha and beta tubulin proteins can bind:", opts:[
["only to GDP",0,"Neither subunit is restricted to GDP. Alpha-tubulin is permanently GTP-bound, and beta-tubulin is added as GTP-tubulin before it hydrolyzes."],
["None of the answers is correct.",1,"Credited: the instructor stresses that the GTP on alpha-tubulin is purely structural and is NEVER hydrolyzed or exchanged, so alpha cannot bind GDP. A blanket statement covering both subunits therefore fails, and no listed option is true for both."],
["to GTP or GDP",0,"True only for beta-tubulin, which has the exchangeable GTP site that hydrolyzes to GDP. Alpha-tubulin is locked in the GTP state, so this cannot describe both proteins."],
["to ATP or ADP",0,"ATP/ADP is the actin nucleotide. Tubulin is a GTPase, not an ATPase."]]},

{n:2, topic:"Myosin", stem:"A genetic form of deafness has been shown to result from a mutation in a myosin gene. This myosin functions as a dimer, is located in the stereocilia of hair cells, and travels toward the minus end of actin fibers to anchor and orient the stereocilia. Which type of myosin would this be?", opts:[
["myosin II",0,"Myosin II is the plus-end-directed muscle myosin that aggregates into bipolar thick filaments; it does not walk toward the minus end."],
["myosin III",0,"Not the minus-end exception. Only one myosin class reverses direction, and it is not myosin III."],
["myosin I",0,"Myosin I is a single-headed (monomeric) membrane-associated myosin, so it is neither a dimer nor minus-end-directed."],
["myosin VI",1,"Correct. Myosin VI is the ONE myosin that moves toward the (−) end of actin, because of an amino-acid insert in its head domain; it works as a dimer and anchors stereocilia in hair cells."],
["myosin V",0,"Myosin V is a two-headed hand-over-hand organelle transporter, but it moves toward the (+) end like all myosins except VI."]]},

{n:3, topic:"Microtubule structure", stem:"Which of the following statements about microtubule structure is incorrect?", opts:[
["Microtubules are polymers of tubulin heterodimers",0,"This is true; the building block is the alpha/beta tubulin heterodimer joined head to tail."],
["All different microtubule structures are nucleated from microtubule organizing centers (MTOCs)",0,"This is true; MTOCs (centrosomes, basal bodies) with gamma-tubulin nucleate microtubules."],
["In a single microtubule, all the protofilaments have the same polarity, with one end having exposed alpha subunits and the other exposed beta subunits",0,"This is true and is the basis of microtubule polarity: alpha faces the (−) end, beta faces the (+) end."],
["The beta subunit of tubulin is constitutively bound to unhydrolyzed GTP",1,"Credited as the INCORRECT statement. It is ALPHA-tubulin whose GTP is constitutive and never hydrolyzed; beta-tubulin holds the exchangeable site and hydrolyzes GTP to GDP after assembly."]]},

{n:4, topic:"Actin-binding proteins", stem:"Which of the following membrane-associated proteins in platelets (thrombocytes) crosslink cellular actins in blood clot formation?", opts:[
["Arp2/3",0,"Arp2/3 nucleates a new branch off the side of an existing filament at 70 degrees; it is a branching nucleator, not a membrane-associated clot receptor."],
["Gp1b-IX",1,"Correct. Gp1b-IX is the transmembrane platelet protein that engages the clot on the outside and links to the actin cytoskeleton inside (via filamin) as the platelet flattens and spreads."],
["filamin",0,"Filamin IS the actin cross-linking protein, but it is cytosolic — it is the partner Gp1b-IX works through, not the membrane-associated protein the question asks for."],
["myosin II",0,"Myosin II is a motor that generates contractile force on actin; it is not a membrane receptor for the clot."]]},

{n:5, topic:"Cell motility", stem:"Lamellipodia are located:", opts:[
["at a moving cell's trailing edge",0,"The trailing edge is where de-adhesion and retraction occur; protrusive actin structures are at the front."],
["throughout the cytosol of a moving cell",0,"Lamellipodia are a specific surface protrusion, not a cytosol-wide structure. Stress fibers run through the cell body."],
["at a moving cell's leading edge",1,"Correct. A lamellipodium is the broad, blunt, branched-actin protrusion at the leading edge; the finer, sharper version is a filopodium."],
["around the entire periphery of a nonmotile cell",0,"That describes the cell cortex or an adhesion belt. Lamellipodia are polarized toward the direction of movement in a motile cell."]]},

{n:6, topic:"Motor protein structure", stem:"The region of a motor protein that interacts with the motor's cellular cargo is the:", opts:[
["head domain",0,"The head is the motor domain: it binds the track (actin or microtubule) and holds the ATPase site."],
["rod domain",0,"The rod/coiled-coil region dimerizes the heavy chains and spaces head from cargo; it is not the cargo attachment site."],
["tail domain",1,"Correct. Cargo specificity lives in the tail: myosin V tails bind organelles, kinesin tails (with light chains) bind vesicle cargo, and dynein's tail links through dynactin to cargo."],
["light chains",0,"Light chains regulate motor activity — in the neck for myosin, in the tail region for kinesin — but the cargo-binding region itself is the tail."]]},

{n:7, topic:"MTOCs and nucleation", stem:"At MTOCs, microtubule nucleation is facilitated by:", opts:[
["centrioles",0,"Centrioles sit inside the centrosome but are triplet-microtubule barrels; the nucleating activity resides in the surrounding pericentriolar material, not the centriole itself."],
["basal bodies",0,"Basal bodies are MTOCs (they template axonemes), but the question asks what facilitates nucleation at an MTOC — that is a protein, gamma-tubulin."],
["γ-tubulin",1,"Correct. Gamma-tubulin is the nucleation factor: it forms a ring scaffold on which alpha/beta dimers start polymerizing, then it leaves. It is not part of the finished microtubule wall."],
["GDP-tubulin dimers",0,"GDP-tubulin is the low-affinity, falling-off state. Assembly requires GTP-tubulin; GDP-tubulin would not seed a new microtubule."]]},

{n:8, topic:"Intermediate filaments", stem:"Which of the following statements about intermediate filaments is false?", opts:[
["Intermediate filaments are characterized by high tensile strength.",0,"True. Their staggered antiparallel packing makes them rope-like and built to resist mechanical/tensile stress."],
["Intermediate filaments have associated motor proteins analogous to the myosins and kinesin/dyneins that travel along microfilaments and microtubules, respectively.",1,"Credited as the FALSE statement. The instructor calls this a \"very very big difference\": there are NO motor proteins associated with intermediate filaments. IFs are not tracks."],
["Intermediate filaments are responsible for nuclear structure and organization.",0,"True. Lamins form the nuclear lamina under the nuclear envelope and maintain nuclear shape and organization."],
["Fungi and plants do not contain intermediate filaments.",0,"True as presented in this course; cytoplasmic IFs are a feature of animal cells."]]},

{n:9, topic:"Myosin", stem:"Which of the following is FALSE?", opts:[
["Organellar transport is accomplished by myosin V.",0,"True. Myosin V is the two-headed vesicle/organelle transporter that walks hand-over-hand toward the (+) end."],
["The myosin with the largest step size is myosin II, which is used for muscle contraction.",1,"Credited as FALSE. Step size scales with neck length, and myosin V has the long neck and the large (~72 nm hand-over-hand) step. Myosin II takes a short step and works in large ensembles in the thick filament."],
["Myosin I is the only family to have a single head domain.",0,"True. Myosin I is built from a single heavy chain and so has one head; myosins II and V are two-headed dimers."],
["Muscle cells contain myosin II, which is responsible for contraction.",0,"True. Myosin II is the muscle myosin that aggregates tail-to-tail into bipolar thick filaments."]]},

{n:10, topic:"Muscle contraction", stem:"Several hours after the death of an individual, a state of rigor mortis; extreme rigidity of the body, Which of the following events contribute(s) to the rigor mortis state?", opts:[
["actin treadmilling",0,"Sarcomeric actin does not treadmill — it is capped by CapZ and tropomodulin and is permanently stable. Treadmilling is irrelevant to rigor."],
["degradation of myosin",0,"Rigor is caused by myosin staying locked ON actin, not by myosin being destroyed. Proteolysis later releases the rigidity."],
["action polymerization",0,"(Typo for actin polymerization.) Muscle thin filaments do not polymerize or depolymerize, and polymerization is not what makes the body rigid."],
["dissociation of Cap Z from the plus end of the actin filament",0,"CapZ loss would destabilize the thin filament; it has nothing to do with the stuck crossbridge that causes rigor."],
["depletion of ATP",1,"Correct. ATP binding is the trigger that releases the myosin head from actin. After death ATP runs out, so every head stays in the rigor state bound tightly to actin and the muscle cannot relax."]]},

{n:11, topic:"Intermediate filaments", stem:"Intermediate filaments are distinct from microtubules in all of the following EXCEPT:", opts:[
["intermediate filaments are not directional",0,"This IS a real distinction. IFs have no plus or minus end, while microtubules are strictly polar."],
["intermediate filaments do not need ATP to assemble",0,"This IS a real distinction. IF assembly requires no nucleotide; microtubules assemble with GTP-tubulin."],
["intermediate filaments are more dynamic",1,"Credited as the EXCEPTION, because it is false. IFs are the LEAST dynamic, most permanent filaments; microtubules show dynamic instability with catastrophe and rescue."],
["intermediate filaments do not have associated motor proteins",0,"This IS a real distinction. Microtubules carry kinesin and dynein; IFs have no motors at all."]]},

{n:12, topic:"Intermediate filaments", stem:"Each protofilament found in the intermediate filament is formed by:", opts:[
["lateral association of parallel dimers",0,"Parallel dimers are only the second step in the hierarchy (monomer to parallel dimer); two of them must first pair up antiparallel before a protofilament exists."],
["lateral association of parallel tetramers",0,"The tetramer is ANTIparallel, not parallel — that is the unique structural feature the instructor says people miss."],
["lateral association of antiparallel dimers",0,"The dimer stage is parallel (N-termini together, C-termini together). The antiparallel arrangement first appears at the tetramer."],
["lateral association of antiparallel tetramers",1,"Credited. The assembly hierarchy is monomer to parallel dimer to ANTIPARALLEL tetramer to protofilament; the antiparallel, staggered tetramer is the unit and is why IFs are non-polar and strong."]],
 note:"The lecture describes antiparallel tetramers associating END TO END into a protofilament, while this key says 'lateral.' No end-to-end option was offered, and the discriminating feature being tested is antiparallel vs parallel and tetramer vs dimer, so the instructor's key is credited."},

{n:13, topic:"Actin-binding proteins", stem:"Addition of which of the following would increase the rate of actin depolymerization on the minus end of the filament?", opts:[
["Cofilin",1,"Correct. Cofilin binds the older, ADP-rich regions of the filament and severs it in chunks, exposing new minus ends and dramatically speeding depolymerization. (Gelsolin does the same job.)"],
["Profilin",0,"Profilin is a POSITIVE regulator: it exchanges ADP for ATP on G-actin so the monomer can be re-added at the (+) end."],
["Thymosine-β4",0,"Thymosin beta-4 sequesters ATP-G-actin monomers in the cytosol. It blocks addition at the (+) end; it does not act on the filament's minus end."],
["CapZ",0,"CapZ caps the (+) end and blocks addition or loss there. It does nothing to the minus end."],
["ATP-G-actin",0,"Adding free ATP-G-actin raises the monomer pool, which favors polymerization, not depolymerization."]]},

{n:14, topic:"Kinesin", stem:"For kinesin motors, the forward of movement along a microtubule is specified by the motor's:", opts:[
["motor (head) domain",0,"The head binds the microtubule and hydrolyzes ATP to generate force, but the head is nearly identical among kinesins that walk in opposite directions."],
["tail domain",0,"The tail (with its light chains in kinesin) binds cargo; it does not set the direction of travel."],
["neck (linker) region",1,"Correct. The neck linker's position and the way it swings forward on ATP binding set directionality — which is why the minus-end-directed kinesin NCD differs from conventional kinesin here, not in the head."],
["stalk domain",0,"The stalk is the coiled-coil that dimerizes the heavy chains and spaces head from cargo; it is structural, not directional."]]},

{n:15, topic:"Actin dynamics", stem:"All of the following statements about actin assembly are correct EXCEPT:", opts:[
["actin (–) ends assemble more rapidly than actin (+) ends.",1,"Credited as the EXCEPTION, because it is backwards. The (+) or barbed end is the fast-growing end (association 12 vs 1.3 per second), which is exactly why treadmilling runs plus to minus."],
["actin subunits can treadmill through an actin filament.",0,"Correct statement. Subunits added at the (+) end appear to move through the filament and are lost at the (−) end."],
["ATP-actin can assemble into filaments.",0,"Correct statement. ATP-bound G-actin is the assembly-competent form added at the barbed end."],
["actin assembly can produce force for movement.",0,"Correct statement. Branched polymerization at the leading edge pushes the membrane forward and propels Listeria."]]},

{n:16, topic:"Myosin", stem:"Which of the following is NOT a function of myosin-powered movements?", opts:[
["cytoplasmic streaming",0,"This IS myosin-powered; myosins dragging organelles and cytosol along actin cables drive streaming."],
["skeletal muscle contraction",0,"This IS myosin-powered; myosin II thick filaments pull on thin filaments in the sarcomere."],
["cytokinesis",0,"This IS myosin-powered; myosin II in the contractile ring constricts the cleavage furrow (its loss gives multinucleated cells)."],
["flagellum-mediated cell motility",1,"Correct. Flagellar beating is microtubule-based: axonemal DYNEIN slides adjacent outer doublets, which nexin links convert into bending. No actin and no myosin are involved."]]},

{n:17, topic:"MT drugs", stem:"The drug taxol acts to:", opts:[
["sever microtubules",0,"Severing/disassembly at the ends is done by proteins such as kinesin-13 and stathmin, not by taxol."],
["promote cell division",0,"The opposite: taxol is an anti-cancer drug precisely because it arrests dividing cells."],
["block microtubule assembly",0,"That is colchicine/colcemid at high concentration, which bind between alpha and beta tubulin and depolymerize."],
["stabilize microtubules against depolymerization",1,"Correct. Taxol stabilizes microtubules so they cannot shorten; the spindle is frozen and chromosomes cannot be pulled poleward, which arrests mitosis."]]},

{n:18, topic:"Cell motility", stem:"Membrane extension during cell locomotion is driven by:", opts:[
["contraction",0,"Contraction (myosin II on stress fibers) retracts the rear and moves the cell body; it does not push the front membrane out."],
["myosin II",0,"Myosin II contributes to cell body translocation and tail retraction, not to the protrusive force at the leading edge."],
["actin depolymerization",0,"Depolymerization at the (−) end recycles monomers but produces no pushing force on the membrane."],
["actin polymerization",1,"Correct. Arp2/3-branched actin polymerizing at the (+) ends directly against the plasma membrane generates the force that extends the lamellipodium."]]},

{n:19, topic:"Actin-binding proteins", stem:"Which of the following statements does NOT correctly match an actin-binding protein with its function?", opts:[
["Thymosin-β4 acts as a buffer for free ATP-G-actin.",0,"Correctly matched. Thymosin beta-4 sequesters ATP-G-actin in dynamic equilibrium with the free pool — exactly a buffer."],
["Profilin acts as a GTPase Activating Protein (GAP) for G-actin.",1,"Credited as the MISMATCH. Actin is an ATPase, not a GTPase, and profilin does not stimulate hydrolysis — it opens the nucleotide cleft so ADP leaves and ATP enters (a nucleotide-exchange role, closer to a GEF)."],
["The Arp2/3 protein complex nucleates the assembly of branched actin.",0,"Correctly matched. Activated by an NPF, Arp2/3 binds the side of a filament and nucleates a 70-degree branch."],
["CapZ bind to the plus ends of actin filaments, preventing subunit addition or loss.",0,"Correctly matched. CapZ is the (+) end cap; in the sarcomere it anchors thin filament length at the Z disk."]]},

{n:20, topic:"MAPs and +TIPs", stem:"MAP2 and Tau are examples of microtubule:", opts:[
["Nucleating proteins",0,"Nucleation is the job of gamma-tubulin at the MTOC, not of MAP2 or Tau."],
["motor proteins",0,"The microtubule motors are kinesin and dynein. MAPs do not hydrolyze ATP to walk."],
["stabilizing proteins",1,"Correct. MAP2 and Tau are classic stabilizing/spacer MAPs; they bind along the lattice, stabilize it, and set inter-microtubule spacing (MAP2 long arm = farther apart, Tau short arm = closer together)."],
["destabilizing proteins",0,"Destabilizers are kinesin-13 (bends and breaks ends) and stathmin (enhances GTP hydrolysis). MAP2/Tau do the reverse."]]},

{n:21, topic:"Cell motility", stem:"When a motile cell wants to change the direction of the movement; it remodels the actin under the cell membrane of the leading/moving edge of the cell.  Which of the following molecular phenomenon is true in the process mentioned?\n\"previous\" denotes where the cell was going before changing direction.\n\"new\" denotes where the cell is trying to go after changing the direction", opts:[
["Capping proteins like tropomodulin caps the minus end of the F-actins in the new leading/moving edge of the cell under the plasma membrane",0,"Wrong end, wrong direction, wrong protein. Tropomodulin caps minus ends (a sarcomere job), and capping the NEW direction would block the growth the cell needs there."],
["Capping proteins like CapZ caps the plus end of the F-actins in the new leading/moving edge of the cell under the plasma membrane",0,"Right protein and right end, but the wrong filaments. Capping the new direction would stop the cell from going where it wants to go."],
["Capping proteins like tropomodulin caps the minus end of the F-actins in the previous leading/moving edge of the cell under the plasma membrane",0,"Correct location but the wrong end and protein: growth is stopped by capping the (+) ends with CapZ, not by capping minus ends with tropomodulin."],
["Capping proteins like CapZ caps the plus end of the F-actins in the previous leading/moving edge of the cell under the plasma membrane",1,"Correct. To turn, the cell caps the (+) ends of the filaments pointing the OLD way with CapZ, so all available ATP-G-actin is funneled into the uncapped plus ends facing the new direction. This happens in milliseconds, e.g. a neutrophil chasing bacteria."]]},

{n:22, topic:"Cell motility", stem:"Listeria is a bacterial parasite that has evolved unique ways to enter animal cells and then use actin polymerization for its intracellular movement. Which is TRUE in regards to Listeria and actin polymerization? (textbook reading required for this question)", opts:[
["Cofilin is necessary to accelerate the assembly of the (–) end of the filament near the tail of the bacteria helping it to propel forward.",0,"Cofilin severs and promotes DISassembly at ADP-rich minus ends; it does not assemble filaments. Assembly happens at the barbed ends pushing the bacterium."],
["Listeria ActA protein binds CapZ and promotes the elongation of the actin filament near the tail of the bacteria helping it to propel forward.",0,"CapZ blocks plus-end growth, and ActA does not work through it. ActA's target is Arp2/3."],
["Listeria ActA activates Arp2/3 complexes and VASP, which together enhances branched ATP-actin assembly near the tail of the bacteria helping it to propel forward.",1,"Correct. ActA on the bacterial surface mimics a nucleation promoting factor, activating Arp2/3 (with VASP) to build the branched actin comet tail whose polymerization propels Listeria cell to cell, hidden from the immune system."],
["Listeria ActA protein binds and inactivates the Arp2/3 complex near the tail of the bacteria helping it to propel forward.",0,"Backwards: ActA is a potent ACTIVATOR of Arp2/3. Inactivating Arp2/3 would abolish branching and stop the comet tail."]]},

{n:23, topic:"Filament comparison", stem:"Microtubules and microfilaments are similar in that:", opts:[
["both have distinct + and – ends",1,"Correct, and the instructor calls this the one real similarity: both are polar polymers with a fast-growing (+) end and a (−) end, and both treadmill."],
["both use GTP as their energy source",0,"A difference, not a similarity. Actin binds and hydrolyzes ATP; tubulin uses GTP."],
["both assemble from a small number of locations in the cell",0,"True only of microtubules, which radiate from MTOCs. Actin nucleates all over the cell, especially at the cortex and leading edge."],
["Both can disassemble by \"catastrophe\"",0,"Dynamic instability with catastrophe and rescue is specific to microtubules; actin does not show it."]]},

{n:24, topic:"Muscle contraction", stem:"What is the function of CapZ and tropomodulin in the sarcomere?", opts:[
["to maintain a constant actin thin filament length",1,"Correct. CapZ caps the (+) end at the Z disk and tropomodulin caps the (−) end in the H zone, so neither end can add or lose subunits — the thin filament is a fixed, permanent length."],
["to make contraction sensitive to Ca2+",0,"That is troponin (the Ca2+ sensor) with tropomyosin, which uncovers the myosin binding sites."],
["to attach actin thin filaments to the Z disk",0,"Anchoring at the Z disk is done by Z-disk proteins such as alpha-actinin; CapZ's role there is capping, not attachment per se."],
["to center myosin thick filaments",0,"Centering and elastic recoil of the thick filament is titin's job, with nebulin running alongside the thin filament."]]},

{n:25, topic:"Myosin", stem:"Which region of myosin interacts with actin filaments?", opts:[
["the tail domain",0,"The tail binds cargo, or in myosin II assembles tail-to-tail into the thick filament's bare zone."],
["the head domain",1,"Correct. The globular head carries both the actin-binding site and the ATP-binding site/ATPase activity that drive the crossbridge cycle."],
["the light chains",0,"Light chains sit on the myosin neck and regulate activity (several are calcium-binding, e.g. calmodulin); they do not contact actin."],
["the rod domain",0,"The rod is the coiled-coil that holds the two heavy chains together; it is structural."]]},

{n:26, topic:"Actin dynamics", stem:"You are studying the rate of actin polymerization and depolymerization in an in-vitro hypothetical system. The G actin concentration in the solution of the experimental setup is 2.5μm.  Based on the data values predict the outcome of the experiment.\nPlease note that X or Y ends can be \"any end\" of the actin filament.\nX end actin: Association rate: 10 μmS-1 ; Dissociation rate: 2 μmS-1\nY end actin: Association rate: 5 μmS-1 ; Dissociation rate: 2.5 μmS-1", opts:[
["The Y end of the actin filament will grow",0,"Y has the HIGHER critical concentration: Cc = 2.5/5 = 0.5 μM. Since 0.5 is much closer to the 2.5 μM G-actin pool than X's Cc, Y is the end that loses subunits comparatively."],
["There is not sufficient data to predict the results",0,"The data are sufficient: Cc = dissociation rate / association rate gives both ends, and you compare each to the free G-actin concentration."],
["Neither X or Y end of the filament will grow",0,"Both Cc values (0.2 and 0.5) lie below 2.5 μM, so the filament is not shrinking overall — net growth does occur."],
["The X end of the actin filament will grow",1,"Correct. Cc(X) = 2/10 = 0.2 μM and Cc(Y) = 2.5/5 = 0.5 μM. Both are below the 2.5 μM pool, and the instructor's rule is that when both are below, the end with the FAR LOWER Cc grows — that is X (the plus/barbed end)."]]},

{n:27, topic:"Muscle contraction", stem:"Which of the following statements is NOT true regarding the role of myosins in muscle contraction? (textbook reading required for this question)", opts:[
["Calcium release from the sarcoplasmic reticulum induces muscle contraction by changing the conformation of actin-binding proteins, allowing myosin binding.",0,"True. Ca2+ binds TnC of troponin, which shifts tropomyosin off the actin filament and exposes the myosin binding sites."],
["Myosin II has a very low duty ratio.",0,"True. Myosin II spends only a small fraction of its cycle attached, which is why muscle needs many heads working as an ensemble (unlike processive myosin V)."],
["Mutations in myosin heavy chain genes can cause hypertrophic cardiomyopathy.",0,"True. Beta-myosin heavy chain mutations are a classic cause of hypertrophic cardiomyopathy."],
["Unipolarity of the thick filaments is crucial for ATP hydrolysis to result in sarcomere contraction.",1,"Credited as NOT TRUE. The thick filament is BIPOLAR: tails associate in the central bare zone and heads project toward both Z disks. That bipolarity is what lets the heads pull the two thin-filament sets toward the center; a unipolar filament could not shorten the sarcomere."]]},

{n:28, topic:"Actin-binding proteins", stem:"Which of the following is a G-actin sequestering protein that allows cells to maintain a relatively high G-actin concentration in cells?", opts:[
["Thymosin β",0,"The specific sequestering protein taught in this course is thymosin beta-4; the generic family name is not the answer the question wants."],
["cofilin",0,"Cofilin is a severing protein that binds ADP-rich filament segments and accelerates depolymerization; it does not sequester monomers."],
["profilin",0,"Profilin binds G-actin but promotes its use: it exchanges ADP for ATP so the monomer can be added at the (+) end."],
["Thymosin β4",1,"Correct. Thymosin beta-4 binds ATP-G-actin and hides it from the assembly pool, so roughly half the cell's actin can stay monomeric without the filaments growing until a signal releases it."]]},

{n:29, topic:"MT drugs", fig:"map-gfp.png", figcap:"GFP fusion MAP (protein X) decorating a microtubule.", stem:"Your lab developed a fusion GFP fusion MAPs (protein X) as shown in the figure for live-cell imaging of microtubule dynamics. You were given the project to do TEM studies on the wild type X and the GFP-X protein. Which of the following reagents you may need for TEM the study?", opts:[
["Taxol",1,"Correct. TEM requires intact, preserved microtubules to image the MAP decorating them, so you need the STABILIZING drug. Taxol locks microtubules against depolymerization during fixation and sectioning."],
["Any of the reagents mentioned can be used",0,"No — the other reagents depolymerize microtubules at the concentrations given, which would destroy the structure you are trying to image."],
["Colcemid",0,"Colcemid binds between alpha and beta tubulin and disrupts microtubules; it is the functional twin of colchicine, not a stabilizer."],
["High concentration of colchicine",0,"High-concentration colchicine DEPOLYMERIZES microtubules (only low concentrations stabilize). You would be left with nothing to view."]]},

{n:30, topic:"Myosin", stem:"All myosins move toward the (+) end of actin filaments EXCEPT:", opts:[
["myosin V",0,"Myosin V walks hand-over-hand toward the (+) end, carrying vesicles and organelles."],
["myosin II",0,"Myosin II heads stroke toward the (+) end of the thin filament, which pulls the Z disks together."],
["myosin I",0,"Myosin I is single-headed and membrane-associated, but still plus-end-directed."],
["myosin VI",1,"Correct. An amino-acid insert in the head of myosin VI reverses it, making it the only (−) end-directed myosin — useful because it moves endosomes from the plasma membrane (plus ends) inward."]]},

{n:31, topic:"Myosin", fig:"myosin-step.png", figcap:"Single-molecule step trace for the recombinant myosin VI; mean step size 73.75 nm.", stem:"You made a recombinant myosin VI by adding 4 amino acids in the neck region of the wild type VI and measured its step size using the plot below. Which of the following statement is most logical?", opts:[
["Wild type myosin VI step size is 75 nm and it steps towards the (−) end of actin",0,"Direction is right, size is wrong. Adding amino acids to the neck INCREASES step size, so the wild type must be SMALLER than the recombinant's 73.75 nm — 75 nm is impossible."],
["Wild type myosin VI step size is 75 nm and it steps towards the (+) end of actin",0,"Both parts fail: 75 nm exceeds the recombinant's measured 73.75 nm, and myosin VI is the one myosin that moves toward the (−) end."],
["Wild type myosin VI step size is 70 nm and it steps towards the (−) end of actin",1,"Correct on both rules. Step size is proportional to neck length, so the wild type (shorter neck) must step less than 73.75 nm — 70 nm fits. And myosin VI is the sole minus-end-directed myosin."],
["Wild type myosin VI step size is 70 nm and it steps towards the (+) end of actin",0,"The step size is plausible, but the direction is wrong: every myosin except VI is plus-end-directed, and this is myosin VI."]]},

{n:32, topic:"Cilia and flagella / IFT", stem:"Which of the following correctly describes the effect that dynein inactivation would have on kinesin-2- dependent intraflagellar transport (IFT)?", opts:[
["Dynein cooperates with kinesin to mediate anterograde IFT, so inactivation of dynein would immediately cease all IFT",0,"Dynein does not power anterograde movement, and the shutdown is not immediate: anterograde continues until the pool of kinesin-2 is stranded at the tip."],
["Anterograde transport is dependent only on kinesin so dynein inactivation would have no effect",0,"This misses the hitchhiking relationship. Kinesin-2 only gets back to the base by riding retrograde cargo moved by dynein, so losing dynein does eventually stop anterograde IFT."],
["Inactive dynein would lead to a transcriptional feedback loop that upregulates kinesin expression and increases IFT",0,"No such compensatory loop is part of the IFT model; the defect is mechanical recycling of motors, not transcription."],
["Dynein is required to recycle kinesin-2 to the base of the flagellum, so IFT would cease after all available kinesin travels to the tip",1,"Correct, and this is the counterintuitive point the instructor flags: each motor depends on the other for its return trip. Lose dynein and kinesin-2 accumulates at the tip, so ANTEROGRADE IFT ceases (and losing kinesin-2 would stop retrograde)."]]},

{n:33, topic:"Motor protein structure", stem:"Which of the following is true of both kinesin and dynein?", opts:[
["Both kinesin and dynein require accessory proteins to mediate transport",0,"Dynein requires the dynactin complex (Arp1, capping protein, dynamitin, p150-glued) to attach cargo; conventional kinesin binds cargo through its own light chains."],
["Both motor proteins carry cargo toward the (+) end of microtubules",0,"Kinesin is plus-end-directed, dynein is minus-end-directed — with no exceptions in dynein."],
["Both kinesin and dynein are very large proteins of >500 kDa",0,"Dynein is enormous (a hexameric ring ATPase, well over 500 kDa); kinesin-1 is much smaller."],
["Both are two-headed motor proteins",1,"Correct. Both are dimeric with two globular motor heads that hydrolyze ATP and walk along the microtubule lattice."]]},

{n:34, topic:"Microtubule dynamics", stem:"Microtubule assembly requires:", opts:[
["ATP",0,"Tubulin assembly runs on GTP, not ATP. (ATP powers the motors that travel on microtubules, not assembly itself.)"],
["microtubule-associated proteins",0,"MAPs stabilize and space existing microtubules, but purified tubulin above its Cc assembles perfectly well without them."],
["incubation at 4°C",0,"Cold does the opposite: chilling a cell depolymerizes microtubules and dissolves the spindle; warming lets them reform."],
["a tubulin concentration in excess of the Cc (critical concentration)",1,"Correct. Exactly as for actin, net assembly occurs only when free subunit concentration exceeds the critical concentration; below Cc the polymer disassembles."]]},

{n:35, topic:"Filament comparison", stem:"Three major groups of filament systems comprising the cytoskeleton are all composed of polymers of assembled subunits, which vary in thickness when assembled. Which is the CORRECT order, from smallest to largest, of the filament systems?", opts:[
["None of the answers is correct.",1,"Correct. The true order smallest to largest is microfilaments (7–9 nm), intermediate filaments (10 nm), microtubules (25 nm) — and no option lists that sequence."],
["microtubules, microfilaments, intermediate filaments",0,"Starts with the LARGEST. Microtubules at 25 nm are the thickest, not the thinnest."],
["microfilaments, microtubules, intermediate filaments",0,"Correct starting point, but microtubules and intermediate filaments are swapped: IFs (10 nm) are intermediate, microtubules (25 nm) are largest."],
["intermediate filaments, microtubules, microfilaments",0,"Fully reversed in the wrong way: it ends with microfilaments, which are actually the thinnest."]]},

{n:36, topic:"Intermediate filaments", stem:"Which of the following does NOT belong to the intermediate protein family?", opts:[
["Laminin",1,"Correct. Laminin is an EXTRACELLULAR MATRIX protein in the basal lamina — the green stain in the intestine image. The instructor flags lamin vs laminin as a commonly missed confusion; LAMIN is the nuclear intermediate filament protein."],
["Desmin",0,"Desmin is a Type III intermediate filament protein of muscle cells."],
["Keratin",0,"Keratins are the most abundant IFs — Type I acidic and Type II basic — in epithelial cells, hair and nails."],
["Vimentin",0,"Vimentin is the Type III intermediate filament protein of mesenchymal cells."]]},

{n:37, topic:"Kinesin", stem:"Which of the following statements regarding kinesin is true?", opts:[
["The domain of kinesin required for microtubule binding is the tail domain",0,"The HEAD (motor) domain binds the microtubule; the tail, with its light chains, binds cargo."],
["The differential affinity of ATP- versus ADP-bound kinesin for microtubules is important for kinesin walking",1,"Correct. The nucleotide state sets track affinity: the ADP-bound head is weakly bound, ATP binding to the leading head swings the neck linker forward, and the alternating affinity cycle is what produces processive walking."],
["The majority of kinesin family members move along microtubules toward the (-) end",0,"Backwards. Kinesins are overwhelmingly (+) end-directed; kinesin NCD is the rare exception, parallel to myosin VI."],
["Kinesin-1 binds to microtubules constitutively, with or without bound organelle cargo",0,"Kinesin is autoinhibited when cargo-free (the tail folds back on the heads), so it does not engage microtubules constitutively — that would waste ATP."]]},

{n:38, topic:"Cilia and flagella / IFT", stem:"The primary cilium:", opts:[
["is relatively susceptible to microtubule destabilizing drugs like colchicine",0,"Axonemal microtubules are stable, modified and long-lived, unlike the dynamic cytoplasmic microtubules that colchicine readily disassembles."],
["None of the answers is correct.",0,"One option is true, so this fails: the central-pair explanation for non-motility is correct."],
["is nonmotile because it lacks the \"central pair\" of microtubules",1,"Correct. A motile cilium has the 9+2 arrangement (nine outer doublets plus two central singlets). The primary cilium has only the nine doublets — 9+0 — so it cannot beat, and instead serves as a sensory antenna."],
["as no known role in humans",0,"(Typo for 'has'.) Primary cilia are important sensors — they detect urine flow in kidney tubules, and defects cause ciliopathies like polycystic kidney disease and Bardet-Biedl syndrome."]]},

{n:39, topic:"Myosin", stem:"In the operational model for the movement of myosin along an actin filament, the power stroke occurs during:", opts:[
["release of phosphate (Pi)",1,"Correct, and the instructor calls this out as the trap: the power stroke happens when inorganic phosphate LEAVES, not when ATP is hydrolyzed."],
[". binding of ATP",0,"ATP binding is the trigger that RELEASES the head from actin — the opposite end of the cycle from force generation."],
["hydrolysis of ATP",0,"This is the answer most students give and it is wrong. Hydrolysis cocks the head and lets it rebind a new actin subunit; ADP and Pi both stay bound and no force is produced yet."],
["release of ADP",0,"ADP release comes AFTER the power stroke and returns the head to the tightly bound rigor state."]]},

{n:40, topic:"Actin structure", stem:"The plasma membrane of eukaryotic cells is supported by:", opts:[
["Actin filaments",1,"Correct. The cell cortex, a dense actin meshwork just beneath the plasma membrane, maintains cell shape and supports structures like microvilli and adhesion belts."],
["Intermediate filaments",0,"IFs provide bulk mechanical/tensile strength through the cytoplasm and at the nuclear envelope, not the membrane-apposed cortex."],
["Microtubules",0,"Microtubules radiate from the MTOC through the cytoplasm as tracks and spindle fibers; they do not form the cortical membrane support."],
["Lamins",0,"Lamins line the INNER NUCLEAR membrane as the nuclear lamina, not the plasma membrane."]]},

{n:41, topic:"Actin dynamics", stem:"Which of the following statements regarding actin assembly in vitro is FALSE?", opts:[
["Large quantities of G-actin can be purified without filament formation as long as the buffer contains low levels of cations.",0,"True. Low salt keeps actin monomeric; adding cations is what triggers polymerization in vitro."],
["During the steady-state phase, the concentrations of F-actin and G-actin are in equilibrium, and no addition to F-actin filaments occurs.",1,"Credited as FALSE. Steady state is DYNAMIC: subunits keep adding at the (+) end at exactly the rate they are lost at the (−) end, so there is no NET change but plenty of addition (this is treadmilling)."],
["Ultracentrifugation can sediment F-actin but not G-actin.",0,"True, and it is the standard assay: polymer pellets, monomer stays in the supernatant."],
["The addition of ATP-G-actin at the (+) end is 10 times faster than at the (-) end",0,"True. Association is 12 μM⁻¹s⁻¹ at the plus end versus 1.3 at the minus end — roughly tenfold."]]},

{n:42, topic:"Microtubule structure", stem:"Where are microtubules observed to be present in different polarities?", opts:[
["Animal cells in mitosis",0,"Spindle microtubules are uniformly oriented with minus ends at the poles (the MTOCs) and plus ends toward the chromosomes/periphery."],
["Animal cells in interphase",0,"The standard interphase array is uniform: minus ends anchored at the centrosome, plus ends toward the cell periphery."],
["Axons of nerve cells",0,"Axonal microtubules are uniformly polarized — minus ends toward the cell body, plus ends toward the terminal — which is why kinesin handles anterograde axonal transport."],
["Dendrites of nerve cells",1,"Correct. Dendrites are the exception: their microtubules are of MIXED polarity, with some plus ends pointed toward the cell body, suiting bidirectional traffic in the receiving end of the neuron."]],
 note:"Near-duplicate of Q67 (same question, options reordered; Q67 opens with 'When are' rather than 'Where are'). Both are kept as written."},

{n:43, topic:"Microtubule dynamics", stem:"Which of the following is not true regarding the dynamic instability displayed by individual microtubules?", opts:[
["Catastrophe describes an abrupt transition of a microtubule from growth to shrinking",0,"True, and it is a microtubule-specific property (with rescue being the reverse transition); actin does not do this."],
["The majority of beta-tubulin subunits in a protofilament are bound to GDP+Pi",1,"Credited as NOT TRUE. Pi is released shortly after hydrolysis, so the bulk of the lattice is GDP-bound beta-tubulin. Only a small region behind the growing tip is GDP+Pi."],
["The protofilaments behind the terminal GTP-beta-tubulin are prevented from peeling back due to lateral interactions with other protofilaments",0,"True. Lateral contacts hold the GDP lattice in its straight conformation; lose the cap and the protofilaments splay and peel."],
["Dynamic instability depends on the presence or absence of a terminal GTP-beta-tubulin cap",0,"True. The GTP cap is exactly what distinguishes a growing microtubule from one undergoing catastrophe."]]},

{n:44, topic:"Myosin", stem:"Multinucleated cells may result from a defect in:", opts:[
["Myosin II",1,"Correct. Myosin II powers the contractile ring in cytokinesis. Delete the myosin II gene or inject anti-myosin II antibodies and nuclear division continues while cell division fails, producing one large multinucleated cell."],
["Myosin I",0,"Myosin I is single-headed and links actin to membranes; it is not the contractile ring motor."],
["Myosin V",0,"Myosin V transports vesicles and positions organelles along actin; losing it does not block cytokinesis."],
["Stress fiber formation",0,"Stress fibers provide adhesion and mechanical stability in a spread cell; they are not what pinches the cleavage furrow."]]},

{n:45, topic:"MT drugs", stem:"You have developed a colcemid-resistant cell line on which you are studying cell division. Which of the following condition will arrest the cell line's cell division in interphase?", opts:[
["A and B are correct",1,"Correct. A (taxol) stabilizes microtubules so the spindle cannot shorten, and B (zero degrees) depolymerizes microtubules so no spindle forms. Both block division, and both work despite colcemid resistance."],
["A and C are correct",0,"C is colcemid, and this cell line is colcemid-RESISTANT, so colcemid will not arrest it."],
["(B) Incubate the cells at Zero-degree Celcius",0,"Cold alone does arrest division, but it is not the only effective condition here — taxol works too, so a single-condition answer is incomplete."],
["(C) Incubate the cells with Colcemid",0,"The whole premise is that these cells resist colcemid, so it has no effect on them."],
["(A) Incubate the cells with Taxol",0,"Taxol does arrest division by over-stabilizing microtubules, but cold does as well, so this single answer is incomplete."]]},

{n:46, topic:"Actin dynamics", stem:"At ATP-G-actin concentrations that are intermediate between the Cc for the (+) end and the Cc for the (–) end, ______________will be observed. The Cc needed for elongation at the (+) end is_____________than that at the (–) end of actin microfilaments.", opts:[
["None of the answers is correct.",0,"One option is fully correct, so this fails: intermediate concentrations give treadmilling, and Cc(+) is lower."],
["shrinking; higher",0,"Both halves fail. The filament is not net shrinking in this window, and Cc(+) (~0.12 μM) is LOWER, not higher, than Cc(−) (~0.6 μM)."],
["treadmilling; lower",1,"Correct. With the pool above Cc(+) but below Cc(−), subunits add at the plus end and are lost at the minus end — treadmilling — and Cc(+) ≈ 0.12 μM is lower than Cc(−) ≈ 0.6 μM."],
["growth; lower",0,"The second blank is right but the first is not: at intermediate concentrations there is no net length change, because loss at the minus end offsets growth at the plus end."]]},

{n:47, topic:"Actin dynamics", stem:"During treadmilling, actin subunits add:", opts:[
["predominantly to filament (+) ends.",1,"Correct. ATP-G-actin associates ~10x faster at the barbed (+) end, and the cytosolic G-actin level sits above Cc(+) but below Cc(−), so addition is essentially all at the plus end."],
["equally to both filament ends.",0,"The association rates differ roughly tenfold (12 vs 1.3), which is precisely why the filament is polar and treadmills."],
["along the length of filaments.",0,"Subunits add only at filament ends. Side binding is what Arp2/3 (branching) and nebulin/phalloidin (stabilizing) do."],
["predominantly to filament (–) ends.",0,"The minus (pointed) end is where subunits are LOST during treadmilling, especially once ATP has been hydrolyzed to ADP."]]},

{n:48, topic:"MAPs and +TIPs", stem:"Which of the following is not an activity of +TIPs?", opts:[
["Linking microtubule (+) ends to membranes",0,"This IS a +TIP activity; plus-end tracking proteins tether growing ends to the cortex and to membranes."],
["Enhancing polymerization at (+) ends",0,"This IS a +TIP activity; EB1 and partners promote growth at the plus end."],
["Enhancing hydrolysis of GTP in the terminal tubulin dimer",1,"Correct — this is the opposite of what +TIPs do. Enhancing GTP hydrolysis destroys the stabilizing GTP cap and promotes catastrophe; that is STATHMIN's job, a destabilizer."],
["Suppressing catastrophes of growing (+) ends",0,"This IS a +TIP activity; by maintaining the GTP cap they keep the microtubule in the growth phase."]]},

{n:49, topic:"MTOCs and nucleation", stem:"The___________________serves as a template for the unusual structure of axoneme microtubules.", opts:[
["centrosome",0,"The centrosome organizes the cytoplasmic/spindle microtubule array; it is not what sits at the base of a cilium templating the axoneme."],
["basal body",1,"Correct. The basal body — a triplet (13+10+10) barrel at the base of a cilium or flagellum — templates the nine outer doublets of the axoneme. (Note the basal body itself has no central pair.)"],
["pericentriolar material",0,"The PCM is the gamma-tubulin-rich matrix around centrioles that nucleates ordinary singlet microtubules, not the doublet axoneme."],
["γ-tubulin ring complex",0,"The gamma-TuRC nucleates individual microtubules generically; the specific 9-fold doublet architecture of the axoneme is templated by the basal body."]]},

{n:50, topic:"Actin-binding proteins", stem:"The two proteins that play the MOST important role in actin microfilament elongation are:", opts:[
["profilin and thymosin β4",1,"Correct. These are the two polymerization regulators: profilin recharges ADP-G-actin with ATP so it can add at the (+) end (positive), and thymosin beta-4 sequesters ATP-G-actin to hold assembly back (negative). Together they control elongation."],
["Capz and cofilin",0,"These are a capping protein and a severing protein — they control length and disassembly, not the monomer supply for elongation."],
["thymosin β4 and cofilin",0,"Only thymosin beta-4 belongs to the elongation-control pair; cofilin severs and promotes depolymerization."],
["cofilin and profilin",0,"Only profilin belongs here. Cofilin is a severing protein grouped with gelsolin for controlling filament length."]]},

{n:51, topic:"Cilia and flagella / IFT", stem:"Which of the following is NOT true about cilia?", opts:[
["All cilia arise from nine sets of outer triplet microtubules (similar to centrioles).",0,"True as taught: cilia grow from basal bodies, which are nine TRIPLET sets just like centrioles. (The shaft itself is doublets.)"],
["All cells with cilia are motile because of the axonemal dynein motor.",1,"Credited as NOT TRUE. Primary cilia are non-motile — they are 9+0, lacking the central pair — and they act as sensory antennae. Having a cilium does not make a cell motile."],
["Cilia contain the same transport system as IFT (Intraflagellar transport) system.",0,"True. Both motile cilia and primary cilia use kinesin-2 (anterograde) and dynein (retrograde) IFT to build and maintain the axoneme."],
["Primary cilia contain receptors that increase the cell's responsiveness to its environment.",0,"True. Primary cilia carry receptors such as the PKD1/PKD2 products that sense urine flow in the kidney."]]},

{n:52, topic:"Actin-binding proteins", stem:"If the activity of thymosin β4 was inhibited in fibroblasts, the overall effect would be:", opts:[
["a decrease in cell locomotion because less actin would be recruited to the leading edge.",0,"Backwards: thymosin beta-4 WITHHOLDS monomers. Inhibiting it releases monomers rather than withholding them."],
["an increase in cell locomotion because there would be a higher concentration of actin available.",1,"Correct. Thymosin beta-4 sequesters ATP-G-actin; remove that brake and the free ATP-G-actin pool rises, so more polymerization occurs at the leading edge and locomotion increases."],
["no change in cell locomotion because thymosin β4 is not involved in activating ActA mediated Arp2/3 activation.",0,"ActA is a Listeria protein and is irrelevant to a fibroblast; also, monomer availability does affect locomotion even though thymosin does not act on Arp2/3."],
["None of the answers is correct.",0,"One option is correct — more free ATP-G-actin means more assembly — so this fails."]]},

{n:53, topic:"Myosin", stem:"Which property is NOT shared by all myosins?", opts:[
["the ability to form dimers",1,"Correct. Myosin I is built from a SINGLE heavy chain and is monomeric/single-headed, while myosins II and V are two-headed dimers. Dimerization is therefore not universal."],
["the ability to bind ATP",0,"Shared by all. Every myosin head is an ATPase; ATP binding and hydrolysis drive the crossbridge cycle."],
["the ability to bind actin",0,"Shared by all. By definition a myosin is an actin-based motor, and the actin binding site is in the head."],
["the presence of a head domain",0,"Shared by all. Every myosin has the globular head containing the ATP and actin binding sites; they differ in tails and neck length."]]},

{n:54, topic:"Actin-binding proteins", stem:"Addition of which of the following would increase the rate of actin polymerization on the plus end of the filament?", opts:[
["profilin",1,"Correct. Profilin opens the nucleotide cleft on ADP-G-actin so ADP leaves and ATP enters, regenerating assembly-competent ATP-G-actin for addition at the barbed (+) end."],
["ADP–G-actin",0,"ADP-bound monomers cannot be productively added at the plus end — they must be recharged with ATP first (that is precisely profilin's job)."],
["Cap Z",0,"CapZ caps the (+) end and blocks subunit addition or loss there, so it would decrease plus-end polymerization."],
["thymosin β4",0,"Thymosin beta-4 sequesters ATP-G-actin away from the pool, lowering the monomer available for plus-end growth."],
["Cofilin",0,"Cofilin severs filaments at ADP-rich regions and accelerates depolymerization from exposed minus ends."]]},

{n:55, topic:"MAPs and +TIPs", stem:"The EB1 protein has several functions. Which statement is TRUE regarding EB1?", opts:[
["It promotes microtubule growth by enhancing polymerization at the (+) end.",0,"True on its own, but incomplete — the other listed statements are also true, so this cannot be the single best answer."],
["Other microtubule plus-end tracking proteins use EB1 to \"hitchhike\" onto the growing microtubule.",0,"True on its own, but incomplete: EB1 is a hub that recruits other +TIPs, yet the remaining statements are true as well."],
["All of the answer choices are correct.",1,"Correct. EB1 does all three: it enhances growth at the plus end, serves as the platform other +TIPs hitchhike onto, and binds back from the very tip as well — it is the green signal in the EB3 kymographs marking growth phases."],
["It can bind further back from the blunt end of microtubules.",0,"True on its own, but incomplete, since the other statements are also accurate descriptions of EB1."]]},

{n:56, topic:"Kinesin", stem:"All of the following statements describe kinesin-I EXCEPT:", opts:[
["kinesin-I binds and hydrolyzes ATP to produce movement",0,"Accurate description. The motor heads are ATPases; nucleotide state sets microtubule affinity and swings the neck linker."],
["kinesin-I transports vesicles along microtubules",0,"Accurate description. Conventional kinesin is the workhorse for anterograde vesicle traffic, e.g. down the axon."],
["kinesin-I is composed of two heavy chains and two light chains",0,"Accurate description. Two heavy chains form the heads and coiled-coil stalk; the two light chains sit in the TAIL region (unlike myosin, where light chains sit in the neck)."],
["kinesin-I is a (–) end-directed motor",1,"Credited as the EXCEPTION. Kinesin-1 is (+) end-directed, carrying cargo toward the cell periphery. The rare minus-end kinesin is NCD, and dynein is the dedicated minus-end motor."]]},

{n:57, topic:"Actin dynamics", stem:"Which of the following statements is FALSE?", opts:[
["The critical concentration of actin for each actin filament is the concentration of free G-actin at which addition and loss are balanced.",0,"True — that is the definition of Cc, the steady-state monomer concentration."],
["Actin treadmilling is caused by the difference in G-actin critical concentrations between the two end",0,"True. Cc(+) ≈ 0.12 μM and Cc(−) ≈ 0.6 μM bracket the cellular ~0.3 μM pool, which is exactly why subunits add at one end and leave at the other."],
["The rate of actin subunit dissociation from a filament is dependent on the concentration of free G-actin.",1,"Credited as FALSE. Dissociation is a first-order process — a subunit falls off at a fixed rate regardless of how much free G-actin is around. Only the ASSOCIATION rate scales with monomer concentration."],
["The actin filament has a gradient of actin bound to ATP, actin bound to ADP-Pi and actin bound to ADP.",0,"True. The newest subunits near the (+) end are ATP-bound, grading through ADP-Pi to ADP in the oldest regions near the (−) end."]]},

{n:58, topic:"Cell motility", stem:"Which of the following is the correct order of events in cell migration?", opts:[
["lamellipodium formation > cell body movement > adhesion > de-adhesion",0,"The cell cannot translocate before the new protrusion has attached — adhesion must precede cell body movement or there is nothing to pull against."],
["adhesion > cell body movement > lamellipodium formation > de-adhesion",0,"Protrusion comes first in the four-step sequence; this puts lamellipodium formation third."],
["adhesion > lamellipodium formation > de-adhesion > cell body movement",0,"Wrong on two counts: extension leads the sequence, and de-adhesion of the rear is the last step, not the third."],
["lamellipodium formation > adhesion > cell body movement > de-adhesion",1,"Correct — the standard four-step cycle: EXTENSION of the lamellipodium, ADHESION of the new front to the matrix, TRANSLOCATION of the cell body, then DE-ADHESION of the rear."]],
 note:"Pairs with Q69, which asks the same sequence as a FIVE-step list beginning with the rear focal adhesion ('step zero'). The two key differently on purpose: a four-step list starts at extension, a five-step list starts at the trailing adhesion."},

{n:59, topic:"Microtubule structure", stem:"A microtubule protofilament is formed by the:", opts:[
["lateral association of only γ-tubulin subunits",0,"Gamma-tubulin is only the nucleation scaffold at the MTOC; it leaves and is not part of the protofilament."],
["lateral association of tubulin dimers",0,"Lateral association is how neighboring PROTOFILAMENTS join to close the 13-protofilament hollow tube — not how a single protofilament is built."],
["head-to-tail association of tubulin dimers",1,"Correct. Alpha/beta heterodimers stack head to tail along a protofilament, which is what creates polarity: alpha exposed at the (−) end, beta at the (+) end."],
["head-to-tail association of only γ-tubulin subunits",0,"Wrong subunit entirely — the microtubule wall is built from alpha/beta heterodimers, with gamma-tubulin acting only as a nucleator."]]},

{n:60, topic:"Myosin", stem:"Which of the following is NOT true of myosin?", opts:[
["Myosin head domains are responsible for both ATP and actin binding.",0,"True. Both the ATPase site and the actin binding site reside in the globular head."],
["Myosin uses the energy released from ATP hydrolysis to do work.",0,"True. That is the definition of a motor protein; the stroke is coupled to the hydrolysis cycle."],
["The tail domains of myosin are responsible for the motor activity.",1,"Credited as NOT TRUE. The tail binds cargo (or, in myosin II, assembles the thick filament). Motor activity — ATP hydrolysis and force generation — belongs to the head."],
["There are 20 distinct classes of myosin proteins in eukaryotes.",0,"True as taught: genomic and bioinformatic surveys indicate roughly 20 classes of myosin, with II, I and V the best characterized."]]},

{n:61, topic:"MAPs and +TIPs", fig:"map-gfp-tem.png", figcap:"Top: GFP fusion MAP (protein X) on a microtubule. Bottom: TEM 1 and TEM 2 micrographs.", stem:"Your lab developed a GFP fusion MAP (known as protein X) as shown in the figure for live cell imaging of microtubule dynamics. You were given a project to do TEM studies on the wild type X and the GFP-X protein. Which of the following is the correct label for the TEM 1 and TEM 2 transmission electron micrograph?", opts:[
["TEM Wild type X; TEM 2: GFP-X",0,"Reversed. Wild type X lacks the bulky GFP, so its microtubules would pack MORE closely — that is the denser micrograph, not the widely spaced one."],
["It can't be predicted from this data",0,"It can be predicted, using the MAP spacer-arm logic: a longer projecting arm holds neighboring microtubules farther apart (MAP2 long vs Tau short is the same principle)."],
["TEM 1: GFP-X; TEM 2: Wild type X",1,"Correct. Fusing GFP lengthens the projecting arm of protein X, so GFP-X spaces microtubules farther apart (TEM 1, loosely packed), while wild type X gives the tighter packing (TEM 2)."]]},

{n:62, topic:"Cilia and flagella / IFT", stem:"The force for axoneme bending is derived from the", opts:[
["Sliding of a central pair of microtubules",0,"The central pair does not slide; it is a structural/regulatory core. Dynein acts between the OUTER doublets."],
["Bending of central pair of microtubules",0,"Again the wrong structure. Removing the central pair gives a non-motile 9+0 primary cilium, but the motor force itself is generated at the outer doublets."],
["Bending of outer doublet microtubules",1,"Credited. Axonemal dynein anchored on the A tubule of one outer doublet grips the B tubule of the neighbor and pulls; because nexin links tie the doublets together, this is converted into bending of the outer doublets, which propagates as the undulating beat."],
["Contraction of outer doublet microtubules",0,"Microtubules do not contract. Their length is unchanged; the beat comes from dynein-driven displacement between adjacent doublets."]],
 note:"Mechanistically the primary force is dynein-driven SLIDING of outer doublets, which nexin cross-links convert into bending; no option offered 'sliding of outer doublets.' The instructor's key (bending of outer doublets) is credited, and the nexin-protease experiment is the evidence: without nexin the doublets slide apart and do not bend."},

{n:63, topic:"Transcytosis", stem:"In the process of transcytosis of the antibodies; the Fc  receptors have _____________for antibodies at pH 6.0 in the____________to capture antibodies; but have ____________for antibodies at pH 7.0 in the ____________to aid the release of the antibodies.", opts:[
["low-affinity, intestinal-lumen, high-affinity, blood or interstitial fluid",0,"Reversed. Capture requires HIGH affinity in the acidic lumen; release requires LOW affinity at neutral pH."],
["high-affinity, intestinal-lumen, low-affinity, blood or interstitial fluid",1,"Correct. The Fc receptor binds IgG tightly at the acidic pH 6.0 of the intestinal lumen, transports it across the cell, and then loses affinity at pH 7.0 on the blood/interstitial side, releasing the antibody."],
["high-affinity, intestinal-lumen, high-affinity, blood or interstitial fluid",0,"If affinity stayed high at pH 7.0 the antibody would never be released on the basolateral side and transcytosis would not complete."],
["low-affinity, intestinal-lumen, low-affinity, blood or interstitial fluid",0,"With low affinity in the lumen the receptor could not capture the antibody in the first place."]]},

{n:64, topic:"Actin structure", stem:"Which of the following statements regarding actin is NOT correct?", opts:[
["Actin cross-linking proteins are responsible for the formation of actin bundles and networks.",0,"Correct statement. Filamin, for example, cross-links branched filaments into networks beneath the platelet membrane."],
["Actin is itself an ATPase.",0,"Correct statement. G-actin has intrinsic ATPase activity, hydrolyzing its bound ATP to ADP + Pi after incorporation, which destabilizes the older filament."],
["At the (+) end of an actin polymer, the ATP-binding cleft is exposed.",1,"Credited as NOT CORRECT. The nucleotide cleft faces the (−) end of the filament, so it is the MINUS end that presents the exposed cleft; at the barbed (+) end the cleft faces inward."],
["Actin exists in a globular form, G-actin, and as a filamentous polymer, F-actin.",0,"Correct statement. G-actin is the soluble monomer and F-actin the polymerized microfilament."]]},

{n:65, topic:"Actin-binding proteins", stem:"What would occur if CapZ was inhibited in cells?", opts:[
["Increased organization of actin in stereocilia due to stabilized actin dynamics",0,"Removing a cap destabilizes, not stabilizes. Uncapped plus ends make the array less, not more, ordered."],
["Faster movement of cells toward a chemical signal",0,"CapZ is what lets a cell steer by shutting down growth in the old direction. Losing it impairs directed turning rather than speeding chemotaxis."],
["Increased rates of polymerization due to reduced availability for ADP-G-actin",0,"Nonsensical as written — CapZ does not control the ADP-G-actin pool. Nucleotide recharging is profilin's job."],
["Increased steady-state treadmilling of F-actin",1,"Correct. CapZ normally blocks addition and loss at the (+) end. Without it, plus ends are free to add subunits while minus ends keep losing them, so the treadmill runs faster."]]},

{n:66, topic:"Filament comparison", stem:"Which of the following statements regarding the three types of cytoskeletal filaments in eukaryotic cells is false?", opts:[
["Intermediate filaments are the only type of cytoskeletal filament not used as tracks by motor proteins",0,"True. There are no IF-associated motors — a point the instructor calls a very big difference between the filament types."],
["Microtubules are polymers of the protein tubulin and are the basis of the mitotic spindle",0,"True. Alpha/beta tubulin heterodimers build microtubules, which form the spindle."],
["Microfilaments are composed of actin and contribute to the shape and organization of the plasma membrane",0,"True. The cortical actin network supports the plasma membrane and shapes microvilli and adhesion belts."],
["Kinesins and dynein motor proteins use actin microfilaments to transport cellular cargo",1,"Credited as FALSE. Kinesin and dynein are MICROTUBULE motors. The actin-based motors are the myosins — microtubules are the highways, actin the local delivery network."]]},

{n:67, topic:"Microtubule structure", stem:"When are microtubules observed to be present in different polarities", opts:[
["Dendrites of nerve cells",1,"Correct. Dendritic microtubules are of MIXED polarity — some plus ends face the cell body — which suits bidirectional transport in the neuron's receiving compartment."],
["Animal cells in interphase",0,"Interphase arrays are uniformly polarized, with minus ends anchored at the centrosome and plus ends toward the periphery."],
["Animal cells in mitosis",0,"Spindle microtubules are uniformly oriented, minus ends at the spindle poles and plus ends toward the chromosomes."],
["Axons of nerve cells",0,"Axonal microtubules are uniformly polarized, minus toward the cell body and plus toward the terminal — the basis of kinesin-driven anterograde transport."]],
 note:"Near-duplicate of Q42; identical content with the options reordered and the stem opening 'When are' instead of 'Where are'. Both retained as written."},

{n:68, topic:"Intermediate filaments", stem:"Which statement is TRUE about intermediate filaments?", opts:[
["Acidic and basic keratins provide dynamic paths on which organelles may travel.",0,"False. IFs are not tracks and have no motor proteins; organelles travel on microtubules and actin. (Acidic/basic refers to amino acid composition, not pH.)"],
["They are named based on their location in cells between actin microfilaments and microtubules.",0,"False. They are named for their intermediate DIAMETER (10 nm, between actin's 7–9 nm and tubulin's 25 nm), not their location."],
["All cells express the same class II cytoplasmic intermediate filament proteins.",0,"False. IF proteins are the most diversified of the cytoskeletal proteins — keratins, desmin, GFAP, vimentin, neurofilaments and lamins are tissue specific."],
["Staggered, antiparallel tetramers give intermediate filaments strength.",1,"Correct. Two parallel dimers pair into a staggered ANTIPARALLEL tetramer; that packing is why IFs have no polarity and why they have high tensile strength."]]},

{n:69, topic:"Cell motility", stem:"The CORRECT order of events in cell locomotion is:", opts:[
["rear focal adhesion, membrane protrusion, front focal adhesion, cell body translocation, de-adhesion",1,"Correct for the five-step version. The instructor calls the trailing adhesion 'step zero': the rear must be anchored to the substrate before a lamellipodium can be pushed out, then the new front adhesion forms, the body translocates, and the old rear adhesion releases."],
["membrane protrusion, front focal adhesion, cell body translocation, de-adhesion, rear focal adhesion",0,"Puts the rear focal adhesion LAST. In the five-step framing the trailing adhesion is the anchor that must already exist before protrusion can generate traction."],
["front focal adhesion, cell body translocation, de-adhesion, rear focal adhesion, membrane protrusion",0,"A front adhesion cannot form before anything has protruded — membrane protrusion must come before the new front attachment."],
["cell body translocation, de-adhesion, rear focal adhesion, membrane protrusion, front focal adhesion",0,"Starts with translocation, but the body can only move after a new front adhesion gives it something to pull toward."]],
 note:"Pairs with Q58, which keys to the four-step sequence starting at lamellipodium formation. This five-step version starts one step earlier, at the rear ('step zero') adhesion, so the two correct answers differ without contradicting each other."},
];
